1. vicsompy — Hedger et al. (2025)
-----------------------------------
Source  : https://github.com/nicholashedger/vicsompy
License : MIT (see vendor/hedger_cf/LICENSE)
Authors : Nicholas Hedger

Files vendored verbatim:
    vendor/hedger_cf/subsurface.py  — Subsurface class
    vendor/hedger_cf/utils.py       — generate_leave_one_run_out

The Subsurface class implements the Laplace-Beltrami Operator Eigenfunction
(LBOE) decomposition of cortical ROI subsurfaces, which is the core
mathematical primitive of the connective field (CF) modeling approach described
in:

    Hedger, N. et al. (2025). Vicarious somatotopy: somatosensory and visual
    representations converge in human cortex during naturalistic movie watching.

generate_leave_one_run_out in vicsompy/utils.py is itself documented as borrowed
from the Gallant Lab / voxelwise_tutorials package (MIT License,
https://github.com/gallantlab/voxelwise_tutorials).  That attribution is
preserved verbatim in vendor/hedger_cf/utils.py.

Our Subsurface59k class (extract_geometry.py) extends Subsurface to load
59k_fs_LR sphere GIFTIs directly and applies a small regularisation shift
(sigma=1e-6) to the sparse eigendecomposition.  The mathematical logic —
Laplace-Beltrami decomposition and eigenvector projection — is unchanged.

The CF modeling pipeline (extract_geometry.py, run_cfmodeling.py,
integration_maps.py, summary.py) is a faithful reimplementation of the
analysis in Hedger et al. (2025), adapted to a unified two-mode (group_average /
per_subject) command-line interface and to the preprocessed CIFTI data format
used in this project.  run_cfmodeling.py combines the data preparation, banded
ridge fitting, and null correction (formerly scripts 02–04) into one parallel-safe
entry point supporting both disk and streaming input modes.


